    <string name="rules_title">How to play</string>
    <string name="rules_1">Black goes first. Take turns placing a stone on any empty point.</string>
    <string name="rules_2">Five in a row, any direction, wins.</string>
    <string name="rules_3">Three in a row with both ends open is an open three: next move it becomes an open four, and that\'s nearly unstoppable. Four with one gap is a four: block it now.</string>
    <string name="rules_4">With Shapes on, those points stand up as light pillars. Teal is your chance, amber is their threat. Taller means more urgent.</string>
    <string name="rules_mistakes">Common slips</string>
    <string name="rules_seg_how">How to play</string>
    <string name="rules_seg_renju">Forbidden</string>
    <string name="mistake_1">Busy building your own line while an amber pillar stands on their side. Block the open three first, then attack.</string>
    <string name="mistake_2">A four isn\'t a win. One block and it\'s dead. Ask what you have after they block.</string>
    <string name="mistake_3">Pinching the moment your eyes land. Wait for the ghost stone to settle on the point you mean. You get half a second to tap it back.</string>
    <string name="mistake_4">Playing Black with forbidden moves on, six in a row doesn\'t win. It loses. Watch the red crosses.</string>
    <string name="rules_renju_title">What are forbidden moves?</string>
    <string name="renju_1">Forbidden moves are a handicap on Black, because going first is a big edge.</string>
    <string name="renju_2">A move that makes two open threes, two fours, or six or more in a row is forbidden for Black. Those points get a red cross.</string>
    <string name="renju_3">With this on, Black needs exactly five to win. We use a simplified check, so it can differ slightly from official Renju.</string>
